<Project>

  <!--
    Pins the output paths to absolute, forward-slash values. This looks like a no-op and is not.

    MSBuild's default BaseOutputPath is the literal string "bin\" - with a BACKSLASH, on every
    platform. DefaultItemExcludes is then built from it, producing entries like "bin\/**". On Windows
    that is a normal path and everything works. On macOS and Linux a backslash is an ordinary
    filename character, so "bin\/**" describes a directory called `bin\` that does not exist, the
    exclude silently matches nothing, and the Web SDK's recursive "**/*" Content and None globs sweep
    bin/ straight back into the build.

    The result is that every build copies the output directory into itself, one level deeper each
    time: bin/Debug/net10.0/bin/Debug/net10.0/... It surfaces first as an MSB3026 "could not copy"
    warning that is easy to read as noise, and eventually as a hard MSB3021 error when the path
    exceeds the OS limit - at which point the tree is also too deep for `rm -rf` to remove.

    MusicSalesApp.ComponentTests already carries an identical file for the same reason. This is a
    cross-platform issue only: the Windows dev box never sees it, which is precisely why it went
    unnoticed long enough to accumulate fifty levels.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BaseOutputPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)bin/</BaseOutputPath>
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)obj/</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Fixing BaseOutputPath alone is necessary but not sufficient, which is worth knowing before
    anyone deletes this half as redundant.

    The SDK builds its own excludes as "$(BaseOutputPath)/**". Because BaseOutputPath conventionally
    ends in a separator, that yields ".../bin//**" - a doubled slash the glob matcher does not match
    against ".../bin/Debug/...". So the exclude is still inert and bin/ is still swept back in, just
    with a tidier-looking path than before.

    This entry is the one that actually bites, and it is deliberately RELATIVE. The SDK's Include
    globs are relative to the project directory, and MSBuild matches Exclude against Include
    textually - an absolute exclude does not cancel a relative include, which is why the obvious
    $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)bin/** spelling looks right and does nothing.

    Set here rather than in the .csproj because Directory.Build.props is imported before
    Microsoft.Common.props, which appends its own entries to whatever it finds - so ours survive.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);bin/**;obj/**</DefaultItemExcludes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The belt to the above braces, because the excludes have not proved sufficient on their own: the
    SDK still resolves Content items under bin once it is populated, and a nested copy reappears
    every few builds.

    Deleting one level before each build is enough to make the problem self-correcting rather than
    cumulative, which is the part that actually hurt - the tree reached fifty levels, broke the build
    with a path-too-long error, and by then was too deep for `rm -rf` to remove. Bounded at one level
    it is a directory nobody notices.

    Cheap, and deliberately not a full clean: wiping bin/obj outright would force a from-scratch
    rebuild every time, which is a far larger tax than the problem. On Windows the directory never
    appears, so this is a no-op there.
  -->
  <Target Name="RemoveNestedOutputCopy" BeforeTargets="Build;Publish">
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(OutDir)bin" Condition="Exists('$(OutDir)bin')" ContinueOnError="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
